0,Tongva - Wikipedia,https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tongva,"Gabrieleño was one of the Cupan languages in the Takic language group, which is part of the Uto-Aztecan family. It may be considered a dialect of Fernandeño, but it has not been a language of everyday conversation since the 1940s.",Gabrielino-Fernandeño,Uto-Aztecan
1,Tongva language - Wikipedia,https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tongva_language,"The Gabrielino language is a subgroup of Takic, a subfamily of Uto-Aztecan, which is usually divided into three subgoups: Serrano-Kitanemuk, Gabrielino (including the Fernandeño dialect) and Cupan. [3] As of 2012, members of the contemporary Tongva (Gabrieleño) tribal council are attempting to revive the language, by making use of written vocabularies, by comparison to better attested ...",Gabrielino-Fernandeño,Uto-Aztecan
2,Gabrielino - California Language Archive,https://cla.berkeley.edu/languages/gabrielino.html,"However, tribal members and language activists have been pursuing language revitalization and reclamation. Gabrielino (also called “Gabrieleño”, “Tongva”, and “Kizh”) is a member of the Takic branch of the Uto-Aztecan language family. Within Takic, it is related to Cahuilla, Cupeño, Juaneño, Kitanemuk, Luiseño, Serrano, and ...·····",Gabrielino-Fernandeño,Uto-Aztecan
3,"Gabrielino Language and the Gabrielino Indian Tribe (Tongva, Kizh ...",https://www.native-languages.org/gabrielino.htm,"Gabrielino Language (Kizh, Tongva) Language: Gabrielino, also known as Kizh or Tongva, was a Uto-Aztecan language of Southern California closely related to Serrano.",Gabrielino-Fernandeño,Uto-Aztecan
4,Gabrielino | Language and Linguistics | Research Starters - EBSCO,https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/language-and-linguistics/gabrielino,"(This is also the case with the name “Fernandeño” for those peoples once surrounding the San Fernando Mission in the present San Fernando Valley, just northwest of urban Los Angeles.) The Gabrielinos are thus closely affiliated with the Fernandeños as part of the Shoshonean branch of the Uto-Aztecan language family.",Gabrielino-Fernandeño,Uto-Aztecan
5,Gabrielino Tongva and Fernandeño Tataviam Band of Mission Indians ...,https://mila.ss.ucla.edu/tongva-approved-primary-sources/,"Places to visit with Fernandeño Tataviam information to destinations and things to do in the northern Los Angeles county Cultivating Reciprocity: The Robert Redford Conservancy, The Tongva Nation, and Pitzer College",Gabrielino-Fernandeño,Uto-Aztecan
6,Fernandeño | Language and Linguistics | Research Starters - EBSCO,https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/language-and-linguistics/fernandeno,"The Fernandeño are a Native American group traditionally located in the northern areas of San Diego County and southern Orange County, specifically the San Fernando Valley in modern-day Los Angeles County, California. They are part of the Tongva peoples and speak a dialect of the Gabrielino language, which belongs to the Uto-Aztecan language family. Historically, the Fernandeño lived in ...",Gabrielino-Fernandeño,Uto-Aztecan
7,Gabrieleño San Gabriel Band of Mission Indians,https://nahc.ca.gov/cp/tribal-atlas-pages/gabrieleno-san-gabriel-band-of-mission-indians/,"Some of enrolled members were the first designated Gabrieleno Most Likely Descendants’ (MLD) in 1979, by the Department of the Interior. Despite the different names, there is only one San Gabriel Band of Mission Indians. The SGBMI has an unbroken historical record. The First Angelinos – The Gabrielino Indians of Los Angeles by William McCawley.",Gabrielino-Fernandeño,Uto-Aztecan
8,Indigenous Peoples of the LA River Basin,https://larivermasterplan.org/about/river-history/indigenous-peoples-of-the-la-river-basin/,"The Uto-Aztecans lived in many different villages, from which multiple distinct nations, lineages, dialects, and identities emerged. Among others, these included the Ventureño Chumash, Fernandeño Tataviam, and Gabrielino Tongva, who lived and continue to live in close relationship with the land and its natural processes. The presence of nomadic communities—and eventually more permanent ...",Gabrielino-Fernandeño,Uto-Aztecan
